{"text": "Che New York Cimes\n\nSATURDAY, MAY 9, 1970\n\nThe Cigarette-Cancer Dispute\n\n\u2018An overwhelming volume of evidence suggests\nthat cigarette smoking is harmful and can lead to lung\ncancer, emphysema, and various heart and circulatory\nproblems, In the current dispute between The Tobacco\nInstitute and the American Cancer Society, however,\nthe latter organization made an error of judgment\nwhich it has now only partially rectified.\nLast February Dr. E. Cuyler Hammond and Dr.\nOscar Auerbach attracted world attention when they\nreported that twelve of 86 beagles trained to smoke\ncigarettes had developed lung cancer. The American\nCancer Society, which helped support this work,\ndeclared the findings \u201ceffectively refute contentions\nby cigarette manufacturing interests that there was\nno cigarette-cancer link.\u201d\n\u2018The Haminond-Auerbach results were path-breaking\nand important, but like all pioneering investigations\nthey must be scrutinized with particular care. That\nthe tobacco companies should want to check the data\nig understandable, and pathologists and statisticians\n\u2018working with those companies should have complete\n{freedom to examine the basic evidence. It was a mis-\ntake for the American Cancer Society to refuse to ask\nthe two scientists to \u201csubmit thelr findings to any\nselected committee chosen by the Tobacco Institute,\nfor any other group.\u201d\nIn the face of public complaint by The Tobacco\nInstitute, the American Cancer Society has had second\nthoughts, Tt has now asked the Surgeon General of\nthe United States Public Health Service to review the\ndata, But the Public Health Service is already on\nrecord as believing cigarette smoking is dangerous\nto health.\nScientific truth is determined ultimately by open\nexamination of data and full discussion by all com-\npetent persons in a field. The Hammond-Auerbach\nresults should be scrutinized intensively by all inter-\n\u2018ested parties. Any other approach throws needless\ndoubt on the already overwhelming case against\ncigarette smoking.\n\nTIMN 0155379",
